Expected Outcomes
A working understanding of how tuned profiles map to concrete sysctl and CPU governor changes on a live system
Two custom tuned profiles (web-server-optimized, database-optimized) created manually and redeployed idempotently through an Ansible role
A reusable benchmarking workflow that captures, compares, and archives performance data across multiple tuned profiles
Troubleshooting
If tuned-adm profile <name> reports success but sysctl values do not change, what would you check first: whether the profile file has a syntax error, whether another service is overriding the sysctl value after tuned applies it, or whether the profile actually includes the sysctl section you expect? How would you confirm which of these is happening?
If the Ansible playbook run reports changed=0 for the "Apply tuned profile" task even after you modified the custom profile's template, what does that suggest about how the command module detects changes, and what would you inspect in the playbook to fix the reporting?
Conclusion
In this lab, students installed and explored the tuned daemon, inspected and applied built-in profiles, and authored two custom profiles tailored to web-server and database workloads. They then automated profile deployment and verification with an Ansible role, playbooks, and inventory structure, and used a Python-based CPU benchmark to capture repeatable, comparable performance data across profiles. Students practiced verifying tuning changes at the sysctl level, generating structured performance reports, and safely rolling back to a default profile. These skills translate directly to enterprise environments, where consistent, automated, and auditable performance tuning across fleets of servers is essential for reliability and predictable workload behavior.
